 const compareFaq=[
 ['What is an effective hourly rate?','It is your total compensation divided by all the time a job takes up — work hours plus commute hours over the weeks you actually work. A $65,000 job can have a lower effective rate than a $60,000 job if it demands more hours or a longer commute.'],
 ['How do I compare two job offers with different salaries?','Enter the salary, expected bonus, weekly work hours, paid time off, and weekly commute time for each offer. Compare the effective hourly rates, then weigh the things this tool can\u2019t put a price on — benefits, growth, and job security.'],
 ['Does a higher salary always win?','No. In the example above, Job A pays $60,000 plus a $2,000 bonus for 40-hour weeks, while Job B pays $65,000 for 45-hour weeks. Job A works out to $27.56 per committed hour against $26.53 for Job B — the lower salary wins on time.'],
 ['How do I compare two hourly jobs with different hours?','Multiply each rate by its weekly hours over the same period. A higher hourly rate doesn\u2019t always win: $18 an hour at 25 hours a week pays $450 a week, while $22 an hour at 20 hours a week pays $440 — the lower rate wins by $10 a week because it carries more hours. Compare the same number of weeks for both jobs, then weigh overtime, paid time off, commute, and benefits on top.'],
 ['Should I include a bonus I might not get?','Run the comparison twice: once with a conservative bonus and once with the full expected bonus. Keep the same assumptions for both jobs so the comparison stays fair.'],
 ['Does paid time off raise or lower the rate?','It raises the effective rate. Paid weeks off cut committed time while the salary stays fixed, so each committed hour accounts for more pay.'],
 ['What does this comparison leave out?','Health insurance premiums and retirement matching are priced in when you enter them above; taxes, equipment, job security, and career growth still aren\u2019t given a value. Treat the effective rate as one input to the decision, not the whole decision.']
 ];
