 section('formula','How does the salary comparison work?',`<div class="formula-card"><div class="formula">Effective hourly rate = (salary + bonus + 401(k) match − health premiums) ÷ [(work hours + commute hours) × (52 − PTO weeks)]</div><p class="example">($60,000 + $2,000) ÷ [(40 + 5) × 50] = $27.56 per committed hour</p></div>`+para('Enter your employer\u2019s 401(k) match percentage and the monthly health premium you pay: the match is added to total compensation and the premium comes off before dividing by committed hours. Leave both at zero to compare salary and bonus only.')+para('A standard salary-to-hourly conversion divides pay by scheduled work hours only. This comparison goes further: it counts every hour the job takes from your week — including the commute — and takes out paid weeks off, when you are paid without committing time.'))
 +section('example','Worked example: when $60,000 beats $65,000',table(['','Job A','Job B'],[['Annual salary','$60,000','$65,000'],['Annual bonus','$2,000','$0'],['Work hours / week','40','45'],['Commute hours / week','5','5'],['Paid time off','2 weeks','3 weeks'],['Committed hours / year','2,250','2,450'],['Effective hourly rate','$27.56','$26.53']],'Gross figures using the tool defaults above. Job B pays $3,000 more per year but commits 200 more hours to earn it.')+para('Job B still puts more money in the bank over the year. The comparison answers a different question: what does each hour of your committed time earn? If those 200 extra hours Job B takes matter to you — for family, rest, study, or a side project — Job A can be the better offer despite the lower salary.'))
 +section('two-offers','Comparing two hourly offers: hours can beat the rate',table(['','Job A','Job B'],[['Hourly rate','$18','$22'],['Hours per week','25','20'],['Weekly pay','$450','$440'],['Pay over 4 weeks','$1,800','$1,760'],['Annual pay (52 weeks)','$23,400','$22,880']],'Gross figures. Job B pays the higher rate, but Job A pays $40 more over four weeks — $520 more over a year — because it carries five more hours a week.')+para('The method is simple: multiply rate by weekly hours, and compare both jobs over the same number of weeks. A higher rate only wins when the hours are close. Before deciding, check the rest of the picture the same way: overtime rules, paid time off, commute, and benefits. Two offers that look close on weekly pay can be far apart once committed time is counted — run both through the calculator above with hours, commute, and paid time off filled in.'))
 +section('four-week-answer','Worked example: $13 an hour vs $10 an hour over 4 weeks',table(['','Job A','Job B'],[['Hourly rate','$13','$10'],['Hours per week','15','18'],['Weekly pay','$195','$180'],['Pay over 4 weeks','$780','$720'],['Annual pay (52 weeks)','$10,140','$9,360']],'Gross figures. Both jobs are compared over the same 4-week window.')+para('Job A wins by $60 over four weeks — $780 against $720 — even though Job B works three more hours a week. The method is the same as in the example above: multiply rate by weekly hours, and compare both jobs over the same number of weeks. Here the extra hours don\u2019t save Job B, because its rate is $3 an hour lower: $15 less every week, $60 less over four weeks, $780 less over a year.')+para('To run your own two offers through the calculator above, first work each hourly offer out as an annual figure the same way: Job A is $195 × 52 = $10,140 a year at 15 hours a week, and Job B is $180 × 52 = $9,360 a year at 18 hours a week. Enter those salaries with their weekly hours (bonus $0, paid time off 0 weeks, commute 0 hours) and the calculator gives effective rates of $13.00 and $10.00 per committed hour — Job A ahead by $3.00 per committed hour. Then add your real bonus, paid time off, and commute to see the full comparison. If you need the annual figure for any other hourly rate first, use the <a href="/hourly-to-salary-calculator/">hourly to salary calculator</a>.'))+section('commute','What a commute really costs',table(['Weekly commute','Hours per year','Value at $25/hour'],[['2.5 hours','125 hours','$3,125'],['5 hours','250 hours','$6,250'],['7.5 hours','375 hours','$9,375'],['10 hours','500 hours','$12,500']],'50 working weeks. The dollar column values commute time at a $25 hourly rate to show its scale; your own effective rate gives your personal figure.')+para('A one-hour round-trip commute every day adds up to about 250 hours a year — more than six full work weeks. A remote or nearby job can quietly be worth thousands more than a higher-paying job across town.'))
 +section('pto','How paid time off changes the rate',para('Paid time off is paid time that commits no hours, so each extra week pushes the effective rate up when the salary is fixed. At $62,000 of total compensation and 45 committed hours a week, the rate rises from $26.50 with no paid time off to $27.56 with two weeks and $28.70 with four weeks. Paid time off is also worth things the rate can\u2019t show: rest, health, and time with family.'))
 +section('beyond','What the rate does not capture',para('This tool prices time, not the whole job. Tuition help, equipment, job security, promotion paths, and how the work fits your life can each be worth more than a small gap in effective rate. Health premiums and 401(k) matching are priced in when you enter them above. Use the rate to see the time trade clearly, then decide with the full picture.')+para('All figures are gross. Taxes differ by income and location and aren\u2019t calculated here. For pay after the deductions you enter, use the <a href="/paycheck-calculator/">paycheck calculator</a>.'))
