 const raiseFaq=[
 ['How do I calculate a salary increase percentage?','Subtract your current pay from your new pay, divide by your current pay, and multiply by 100. A move from $60,000 to $66,000 is ($66,000 − $60,000) ÷ $60,000 × 100 = 10%.'],
 ['How much is a 10% raise on $60,000?','$6,000 more per year — $500 a month, about $115.38 a week, or $2.88 an hour at 40 hours a week.'],
 ['What is a good salary increase?','It depends on inflation, your role, and the market. A raise that matches inflation keeps your purchasing power flat; anything above it is a real increase in what your pay can buy. This tool does the arithmetic — it doesn\u2019t tell you what to ask for.'],
 ['How do I calculate a raise for hourly pay?','Switch the pay basis to hourly wage and enter both rates. A move from $25 to $27.50 an hour is a 10% increase.'],
 ['Does a 10% raise mean 10% more take-home pay?','Not exactly. The figures here are gross, and taxes and percentage-based deductions can change the take-home difference.'],
 ['Can this calculator show a pay cut?','Yes. If the new pay is lower than the current pay, the change is negative — for example, $60,000 to $57,000 is a -5% change.'],
 ['Can I calculate a new salary from a raise percentage?','Yes. Switch the calculation mode to “I know the raise %” and enter your current pay and the percentage. A 10% raise on $60,000 gives $66,000.']
 ];
