 const freelanceFaq=[
 ['What counts as a billable hour?','An hour a client pays for. Leave room for admin, proposals, marketing, and other work that doesn\u2019t generate a direct charge. Many freelancers bill only half to two-thirds of their working time.'],
 ['Is the tax reserve a tax estimate?','No. You enter your own reserve percentage as a planning assumption. Real tax depends on your circumstances and where you live — the tool doesn\u2019t work it out.'],
 ['Why is a freelance rate so much higher than an employee wage?','An employee wage comes with employer-paid benefits, payroll tax contributions, paid leave, and equipment. A freelancer pays for all of that out of the rate, and only gets paid for billable hours — so the rate has to cover far more than the paycheck alone.'],
 ['How many billable hours should I plan for?','Start from your real week. If you work 40 hours and spend 15 on admin, marketing, and communication, 25 billable hours is a realistic plan. Overestimating billable hours is the most common reason a target rate comes out too low.'],
 ['What profit margin should I use?','The margin is the share of revenue kept as business profit after income, the tax reserve, and expenses. Ten to twenty percent is a common planning range. It is a share of revenue — not a markup on costs.'],
 ['Does this rate guarantee I will hit my income goal?','No — it is the rate your plan needs. Whether you earn it depends on winning enough work at that rate. If the market won\u2019t support the rate, the honest options are more billable hours, lower expenses, or a lower income target.'],
 ['What is self-employment tax?','It is the Social Security and Medicare tax a freelancer pays on net earnings: 15.3% applied to 92.35% of net earnings, or about 14.13% effective, before income tax. That is why a 25–30% tax reserve is a common planning range.']
 ];
 const freelanceBody=quick('<strong>$79.63 an hour is the target rate</strong> for a $60,000 take-home goal with $6,000 of expenses, a 25% tax reserve, a 10% profit margin, and 1,200 billable hours a year.')
 +layoutPin('Price your freelance time',[['formula','The rate formula'],['billable','Billable hours decide the rate'],['employee','Freelancer rate vs. employee wage'],['reserve','Tax reserve & profit margin'],['related','Related calculators'],['tool-faqs','Questions & methodology']],
 section('formula','How is a freelance rate calculated?',`<div class="formula-card"><div class="formula">Target revenue = [income goal ÷ (1 − tax reserve) + expenses] ÷ (1 − profit margin)</div><p class="example">[$60,000 ÷ 0.75 + $6,000] ÷ 0.90 = $95,556 of required revenue — then ÷ 1,200 billable hours = $79.63/hour</p></div>`+para('The math works backwards from your life, not forwards from a market guess. First your take-home goal is bumped up to leave room for tax. Then expenses are added. Then the total is bumped up again so profit is left over after everything else is paid. Only then is it divided by the hours clients will actually pay for.'))
 +section('billable','Billable hours decide the rate',table(['Billable hours / week','Billable hours / year','Required rate'],[['15','720','$132.72'],['20','960','$99.54'],['25','1,200','$79.63'],['30','1,440','$66.36'],['35','1,680','$56.88'],['40','1,920','$49.77']],'Same $95,556 revenue target at 48 billable weeks. Halving your billable hours doubles the rate you need — which is why realistic hours matter more than any other input.')+para('Admin, proposals, marketing, invoicing, and learning are real work that no client pays for directly. If you work 40 hours a week but can only bill 25 of them, plan on 25. A rate built on hours you can\u2019t bill will miss your income goal even if every client says yes.'))
 +section('employee','Why a freelance rate is not an employee wage',para('A $60,000 salary works out to about $28.85 an hour — yet the same $60,000 take-home goal needs a $79.63 freelance rate in this example. The gap isn\u2019t greed; it is everything employment normally covers: employer payroll taxes, health insurance, retirement contributions, paid vacation and sick days, equipment, software, and the unpaid hours between projects. Your rate has to pay for all of it.')+para('Comparing a freelance rate with a salary hourly equivalent without those costs is the most common pricing mistake new freelancers make. Use the <a href="/salary-to-hourly-calculator/">salary to hourly calculator</a> to see the employee side of the comparison.'))
 +section('reserve','Tax reserve and profit margin',para('The tax reserve is a planning set-aside, not a tax calculation: at 25%, the model keeps $20,000 of the $80,000 grossed-up income aside so $60,000 is left. Your real reserve depends on your tax situation — many freelancers set aside 25–30%. The profit margin is different from profit on a single job: it is the share of all revenue the business keeps, which pays for slow months, new equipment, and growth.')+para('Self-employment tax is why the reserve runs high: it is 15.3% on 92.35% of net self-employment earnings (about 14.13% effective) before income tax. A 25–30% reserve covers that plus federal and state income tax for many freelancers. The calculator also shows your day rate — the hourly rate × 8 — for day-based quotes, and a monthly retainer (required revenue ÷ 12) for retainer quotes.'))
 +section('related','Related calculators',related('freelance-rate'))
 +section('tool-faqs','Frequently asked questions',faqHtml(freelanceFaq))
 +section('method','Freelance rate methodology',methodology('The income goal is divided by (1 − tax reserve), expenses are added, and the result is divided by (1 − profit margin) to get required revenue. The rate is required revenue divided by billable hours (hours per week × weeks per year). Day rate is the hourly rate × 8. Monthly retainer is required revenue ÷ 12.')));
 const bonusFaq=[
 ['Is a bonus taxed at 40%?','There is no single 40% bonus tax rate. What comes out of the check is withholding: 22% federal, 7.65% FICA, plus your state rate where one applies — which can add up to 35–40% withheld. The final tax on a bonus is your ordinary marginal rate, settled when you file.'],
 ['Why is my bonus taxed so high?','Because withholding isn\u2019t the final tax. Payroll takes the flat 22% federal rate (or the aggregate tables) plus Social Security and Medicare out of one payment, with no standard deduction taken off first. If that overshoots your real marginal rate, the extra comes back as a refund.'],
 ['Do I get bonus tax back?','You get back only the part that was over-withheld. If your marginal rate is below 22%, the flat withholding took too much and the difference comes back at filing. If your marginal rate is above 22%, you may owe the difference instead.'],
 ['What is the aggregate method for bonus withholding?','Your employer adds the bonus to one regular paycheck, works out withholding on the combined amount through the IRS percentage-method tables, and takes off the withholding on the regular pay alone. The rest comes out of the bonus. It is the employer’s choice, not yours.'],
 ['Does a 401(k) contribution reduce bonus tax?','It lowers the federal withholding base — not the final tax directly, and never FICA. Putting $1,000 of a $10,000 bonus into a 401(k) cuts flat-method federal withholding from $2,200 to $1,980 — $220 less withheld — while Social Security and Medicare still apply to the full $10,000.'],
 ['Are bonuses taxed differently than salary?','No. A bonus is ordinary income, taxed at the same rates as salary when you file. Only the withholding during the year is different, because bonuses are supplemental wages under IRS Publication 15.']
 ];
 const bonusBody=quick('<strong>A $5,000 bonus pays $3,517.50 after withholding.</strong> The flat method takes 22% for federal tax plus 7.65% for Social Security and Medicare, before any state withholding.')
 +layoutPin('Understand your bonus tax',[['formula','How bonus tax is calculated'],['flat-rate','The 22% rule & the $1M tier'],['amounts','Take-home by bonus size'],['aggregate','Flat vs aggregate method'],['fica','FICA & the wage cap'],['retirement','A 401(k) from your bonus'],['final-tax','Withholding vs final tax'],['related','Related calculators'],['tool-faqs','Questions & methodology']],
 section('formula','How is bonus tax calculated?',`<div class="formula-grid"><div class="formula-card"><h3>Federal withholding — flat method</h3><div class="formula">22% of the bonus up to $1M of supplemental wages, then 37%</div><p class="example">$5,000 × 22% = $1,100 federal withholding</p></div><div class="formula-card"><h3>FICA — Social Security & Medicare</h3><div class="formula">6.2% up to the $184,500 wage base + 1.45% Medicare on the full bonus</div><p class="example">$5,000 × 7.65% = $382.50 when wages are under the cap</p></div></div>`+para('Bonuses count as supplemental wages in IRS Publication 15. When a bonus is paid separately from regular pay, the employer can withhold at the flat 22% rate; when it is paid together with regular pay, the employer can use the aggregate method instead. Either way, Social Security and Medicare come out on top, and any state rate you enter applies to the full bonus. Our guide to <a href="/articles/how-are-bonuses-taxed/">how bonuses are taxed</a> walks through the same rules with a full-year example.'))
 +section('flat-rate','The 22% rule and the $1M tier',para('The 22% rate applies to supplemental wages up to $1,000,000 from one employer in one calendar year. Below that line, the employer can choose the flat percentage method or the aggregate method. Above $1,000,000 of supplemental wages in the year, withholding at 37% on the excess is mandatory, whichever method is used for the rest.')+box('<strong>Example:</strong> a $1,500,000 bonus with no earlier bonuses withholds $220,000 on the first $1,000,000 (22%) plus $185,000 on the remaining $500,000 (37%) — $405,000 of federal withholding before FICA.')+para('Bonuses already paid by the same employer earlier in the year count toward the $1,000,000. That is why the calculator asks about bonuses already paid: a $200,000 bonus after $900,000 of earlier bonuses crosses the tier, and $37,000 of it is withheld at 37%.'))
 +section('amounts','Take-home by bonus size',table(['Bonus','Federal 22%','FICA 7.65%','Take-home'],[[money(1000,0),money(220),money(76.5),money(703.5)],[money(2500,0),money(550),money(191.25),money(1758.75)],[money(5000,0),money(1100),money(382.5),money(3517.5)],[money(10000,0),money(2200),money(765),money(7035)],[money(25000,0),money(5500),money(1912.5),money(17587.5)],[money(50000,0),money(11000),money(3825),money(35175)]],'Flat method, no state withholding, and wages below the Social Security wage base. Add your state rate above to see your own figures.'))
 +section('aggregate','Flat vs aggregate: which method applies?',table(['Flat (percentage) method','Aggregate method'],[['Bonus paid separately; 22% withheld up to $1M, 37% above','Bonus added to one regular paycheck and run through the IRS tables'],['The same rate at every income level','The withholding follows your pay, frequency, and filing status'],['Simple to predict: bonus × 22%','Can withhold more or less than 22%, depending on the combined check']],'','vs-table')+para('With a $10,000 bonus paid on top of a $2,000 biweekly paycheck (single filer), the aggregate method withholds $2,625.93 of federal tax — more than the flat method’s $2,200 — because the combined check is treated as if every check were that large. Take-home is $6,609.07 against $7,035.00 flat. You can\u2019t choose the method; the employer decides for each payment, and many payroll systems always use one of the two.'))
 +section('fica','How FICA applies to a bonus',para('Social Security is 6.2% of wages up to the 2026 wage base of $184,500 — and your regular wages use up the base first. If you have already earned $180,000 this year, only $4,500 of room is left, so a $10,000 bonus owes Social Security on $4,500 ($279.00) and Medicare on the full $10,000 ($145.00). Medicare has no cap, and an extra 0.9% Additional Medicare Tax is withheld on wages above $200,000.')+table(['Wages before the bonus','Bonus','Social Security','Medicare + additional'],[[money(0,0),money(10000,0),money(620),money(145)],[money(100000,0),money(10000,0),money(620),money(145)],[money(180000,0),money(10000,0),money(279),money(145)],[money(190000,0),money(20000,0),money(0),money(380)]],'The last row includes $90 of Additional Medicare Tax on the $10,000 of wages above $200,000.'))
 +section('retirement','What a 401(k) contribution does to a bonus',para('A pre-tax 401(k) deferral taken out of the bonus lowers the federal withholding base dollar for dollar — but it never lowers FICA, which is charged on the full bonus. On a $10,000 bonus, deferring $1,000 cuts flat-method federal withholding from $2,200 to $1,980, while the $765 of FICA stays the same; $6,255 reaches your account and $1,000 goes into the plan.')+para('Whether a bonus can carry a 401(k) election depends on the plan: some employers apply your normal deferral percentage to bonuses, others leave bonus pay out or use a separate election. See what a contribution does to a regular check with the <a href="/calculators/401k-impact/">401(k) paycheck impact calculator</a>.'))
 +section('final-tax','Withholding is not the final tax',para('When you file, the bonus is taxed as ordinary income at your marginal rate — the same as salary. The 22% withheld is a prepayment. If your marginal rate is 12%, too much was withheld and the difference comes back in your refund; if it is 32%, you under-withheld and may owe — a Form W-4 extra-withholding entry can fix that before year-end.')+box('<strong>The refund test:</strong> compare 22% with your marginal bracket. Below 22%, expect some of the withholding back. Above it, plan for a smaller refund or a balance due. The guide to <a href="/articles/how-are-bonuses-taxed/">how bonuses are taxed</a> walks through the full-year projection step by step.'))
 +section('related','Related calculators',related('bonus-tax'))
 +section('tool-faqs','Frequently asked questions',faqHtml(bonusFaq))
 +section('methodology','Bonus tax methodology',methodology('The flat method withholds 22% of the bonus after any pre-tax 401(k) deferral, up to $1,000,000 of supplemental wages per employer per year, and 37% on the part above. The aggregate method annualizes regular pay plus the bonus through the IRS percentage method (2026 brackets and standard deduction, standard Form W-4) and subtracts the withholding on regular pay alone. Social Security is 6.2% of the part of the bonus under the $184,500 wage base after year-to-date wages; Medicare is 1.45% of the full bonus plus 0.9% on wages above $200,000. State withholding is the flat rate entered, applied to the full bonus.')));
